 ],
 "literal": "Birds also lamented, flowers here closed / the seashore grove also possesses much loneliness, the sky also / resembling us indeed, having become distressed / as daylight passes, it became dim indeed / I still exist indeed, O friend, in this state / cool, fragrant ñāḻal tree / if only I could find those who would tell the lord of the cool, beautiful shore",
 "translation": "The birds have fallen silent and lament, and the flowers here have closed. The seashore grove, too, is filled with deep loneliness. The sky, resembling us, has grown distressed and dim as daylight fades away. Yet, O friend, I still survive in this state. If only I could find someone to go and tell the lord of the cool, beautiful shore, where the cool ñāḻal trees bloom with fragrance!",
 "modern": "பறவைகளும் வருந்தி ஒலித்தன; இங்குள்ள பூக்களும் குவிந்தன. கடற்கரைச் சோலையும் மிகுந்த தனிமையைக் கொண்டுள்ளது. வானமும் நம்மைப் போலவே மயக்கமுற்று, பகல் பொழுது கழிந்தவுடன் பொலிவிழந்துவிட்டது. தோழியே! குளிர்ந்த, நறுமணம் வீசும் ஞாழல் மரங்கள் நிறைந்த குளிர்ந்த கடற்கரைத் தலைவனுக்கு என் நிலையைச் சொல்லக்கூடிய ஒருவரைப் பெற்றால், இந்நிலையிலும் நான் உயிர் பிழைத்திருப்பேன்.",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her confidante during the evening, expressing her deep grief over the hero's absence. This poem belongs to the neytal (seashore) landscape, which traditionally depicts the theme of pining and lamentation in separation. As evening falls, the natural world around her reflects her own sorrow: the birds fall silent, the flowers close, and the sky grows dim. She compares the darkening sky to her own distressed mind. She laments that she is still barely holding onto life in this state, and wishes she could find a messenger to convey her suffering to her lover, the lord of the cool, beautiful shore where fragrant ñāḻal trees grow.",
